==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|la|en}} {{m|la|exquīsītus}}, perfect passive participle of {{m|la|exquīrō||seek out}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en-us-exquisite.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/ɪkˈskwɪzɪt/|/ˈɛkskwɪzɪt/|lang=en}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Especially [[fine]] or [[pleasing]]; [[exceptional]].
#: {{ux|en|They sell good coffee and pastries, but their chocolate is '''exquisite'''.}}
#: {{ux|en|Sourav Ganguly scored an '''exquisite''' century in his debut Test match.}}
#* {{RQ:Chmbrs YngrSt|I}}
#*: Selwyn, sitting up rumpled and cross-legged on the floor, after having boloed Drina to everybody's '''exquisite''' satisfaction, looked around at the sudden rustle of skirts to catch a glimpse of a vanishing figure&mdash;a glimmer of ruddy hair and the white curve of a youthful face, half-buried in a muff.
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} Carefully adjusted; precise; accurate; exact.
# [[recherché|Recherché]]; [[far-fetched]]; [[abstruse]].
# Of special [[beauty]] or rare [[excellence]].
# Exceeding; extreme; keen, in a bad or a good sense.
#: {{ux|en|'''exquisite''' pain or pleasure}}
# Of delicate perception or close and accurate discrimination; not easy to satisfy; exact; fastidious.
#: {{ux|en|'''exquisite''' judgment, taste, or discernment}}
#* {{w|Thomas Fuller}} (1606-1661)
#*: his books of Oriental languages, wherein he was '''exquisite'''

====Synonyms====
* [[beautiful]], [[delicate]], [[discriminating]]

